---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { bountyRules, insiderRules, money, rulesVersion, shape, site } from '../../config/site';
import { attempt, getOrganizerPledge } from '../../lib/db';
import { WAYS } from '../../lib/ways';

const e = site.eligibility;
const dollars = (n: number) => '$' + n.toLocaleString('en-US');
// My own pledge, read from the database like anyone else's. If it can't be read, the page says nothing about it.
const pledged = await attempt(getOrganizerPledge);
// Built as whole strings so no number ends up glued to the word before it.
const mine = pledged ? `I've pledged ${dollars(pledged / 100)} to the pool, from my own money.` : '';
const checks = `You sign in with GitHub or GitLab. The site reads two things from your profile and shows you each result: your account is at least ${e.minAccountAgeDays} days old, and your profile shows at least ${e.minContributions12mo} contributions in the last 12 months.`;
const ideas = `Anyone signed in can post an idea for something that should be free, up to ${site.ideas.maxPerPerson} each.`;
const held = `A pledge is listed straight away only when it is from an individual, is ${dollars(site.pledgeHold.aboveUsd)} or less, and shows the pledger's own username. Anything else (a company, a larger amount, a different display name, an X account) is checked by a person first.`;
---

<Base title="About" description="What Antipaywall is, who is behind it, what he is putting in, and exactly how it works.">
  <h1 class="page-title">What this is, and how it works.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Antipaywall is happening. This page lays out exactly how it works, who is behind it, and the
    few things still being finalised before the dates are announced.
  </p>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      A free, one-week hackathon for people who build software. The brief is one line: build the
      free, open-source version of something people would otherwise pay for. One feature of a big
      product is a fair scope. Every entry has to be open source, in a public GitHub repository.
    </p>
    <p>{shape.summary} {shape.when}</p>

    <h2>Who I am</h2>
    <p>
      I'm Ethan Rogers. I've been an entrepreneur for ten years, mostly running marketing and
      web development agencies, and I was writing code for a living before AI could write it
      for you. These days I build software that runs on AI agents, and several of my projects
      are open source.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'm one of the builders this is for. You can see what I make at{' '}
      <a class="link" href="https://ethanplus.ai" rel="noopener">ethanplus.ai</a>, on{' '}
      <a class="link" href="https://github.com/ethanplusai" rel="noopener">GitHub</a> and on{' '}
      <a class="link" href="https://x.com/ethanplusai" rel="noopener">X</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Why I'm doing it</h2>
    <p>
      I think AI is making software cheap to build, and that most of it is heading toward open
      source. If you believe something is valuable, give people the thing itself. If it really
      is valuable, the demand will show, and a business can still grow from it.
    </p>
    <p>
      I wanted a hackathon built around that, and run the way I'd want one run as a builder. I'm
      doing it either way. How big it gets depends on who joins. A hackathon owes its builders a
      few plain things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>It should be free to enter, with nothing sold to you once you are in.</li>
      <li>The prize money should be held by someone other than the person running it.</li>
      <li>The rules should be public and fixed before anyone starts building.</li>
      <li>The people who built should be the ones who decide who won, in the open.</li>
      <li>Being disqualified should take a written rule, a published reason and a chance to appeal.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Antipaywall is my attempt to run one that way, and to make each of those things something you can check rather than something you have to take my word for.</p>

    <h2>What I'm putting in</h2>
    <p>
      {mine} I'm doing the work of bringing in sponsors, and I'm asking them to cover the pool
      holder's fee, so what people give reaches the pool whole. I take nothing out: no cut, no
      fee, no salary, no profit.
    </p>
    {
      mine && (
        <p>
          That money is a pledge today, like anyone else's, because there is nowhere to pay it
          yet. I pay it when the pool opens.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <h2>What I'm asking for</h2>
    <p>Help. One person can't run this fairly alone, and it isn't worth running without builders. There are five ways in, and the first one costs nothing:</p>
    <ul>
      {WAYS.map((w) => <li><strong>{w.label}.</strong> {w.blurb}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p><a class="link" href="/hackathon/join">Pick one on the join page.</a></p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>

    <h3>Joining</h3>
    <p>
      {checks} Pass both and you can join as a builder. The checks make throwaway accounts
      more expensive to create. They are not proof of who anyone is, and they run again each
      time you sign in.
    </p>

    <h3>Ideas and hearts</h3>
    <p>
      {ideas} When you post one, the site searches GitHub for open-source
      projects that already do it and asks what they are missing. Anyone can heart an idea, with
      no account. Hearts show builders what people want. They carry no money and no weight in who
      wins, and a determined person could inflate them.
    </p>

    <h3>The pool, and who holds it</h3>
    <p>{money.full}</p>
    <p>
      The prize pool is whatever the community and sponsors put in. It is split across nine
      winners: 30%, 18% and 12% for the top three, 6% each for the next five, and 10% for
      Community Choice.
    </p>

    <h3>Pledges are not payments</h3>
    <p>
      Until the pool opens, the only thing anyone can do is pledge: a public promise of an amount.
      Pledges are listed with the pledger's GitHub or GitLab username and are never counted as money in the
      pool. {held} When the pool opens, people who pledged come back and pay.
    </p>

    <h3>Sponsor bounties</h3>
    <p>A sponsor can put up money for the best entry that does one specific thing. The first ones will be listed as sponsors commit. This is how they will be decided:</p>
    <ul>
      {bountyRules.map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h3>The vote</h3>
    <p>
      Winners will be chosen by the builders. Everyone who submits an eligible entry will get
      three votes, for three different projects, none of them their own. Every ballot will be
      published, so anyone can see who voted for what. Community Choice will be the one prize
      decided by a wider vote, open to any GitHub or GitLab account that passes the same two checks.
      Sponsors will get no vote on any prize. I will get no vote on the main prizes, and neither
      will the review panel.
    </p>

    <h3>The review panel and appeals</h3>
    <p>
      A panel of named volunteers will check flagged entries and the top ten. It will be able to
      disqualify an entry only for breaking a written rule, it will have to publish its
      reasoning, and it will not rank anyone. A disqualified builder will have 72 hours to
      appeal in public. Applications for the panel are open now, and its members are listed
      here once they are chosen.
    </p>

    <h3>Those of us running it</h3>
    <p>
      I plan to build an entry myself, and I'd like the review panel to as well. So the rule is:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {insiderRules.map((r) => <li>{r}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <h3>Getting paid</h3>
    <p>
      Once the panel has cleared the winners, they will be paid from the pool by its holder,
      and each payout will be public. If a winner can't be paid, their share goes to the next
      place.
    </p>

    <h2>How you can check it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The numbers on this site</strong> (builders, pledges, hearts, ideas) are counted from its database when the page loads. If they can't be read, the page says so instead of showing a zero. The one exception is sponsors: each sponsor's amount, and whether it is pledged or paid, is entered by me from what that sponsor has committed or paid.</li>
      <li><strong>The rules</strong> are <a class="link" href="/hackathon/rules">published on this site</a>, version {rulesVersion}. Every change is listed there with its date, and because this site's code is public, the full history of the page is too.</li>
      <li><strong>The money</strong> will sit on a public ledger kept by the holder, showing every contribution and every payout.</li>
      <li><strong>The ballots</strong> will be published in full.</li>
      <li><strong>What the site records about you</strong> is listed on the <a class="link" href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>The code that runs this site</strong> is public. Read it at <a class="link" href={site.repo} rel="noopener">github.com/antipaywall/platform</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What's being finalised</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The dates.</strong> {shape.when}</li>
      <li><strong>The pool holder.</strong> {money.holder}</li>
      <li><strong>The review panel.</strong> Applications are open. If you'd do it, <a class="link" href="/hackathon/join/panel">apply</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>The rest of the site.</strong> Submitting an entry, the checks on entries, voting and payouts get built before build week starts.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What I'm committing to</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It is free to enter, and nothing will be sold to you for entering.</li>
      <li>I take nothing out of it.</li>
      <li>I never hold the pool.</li>
      <li>Nobody is asked to pay anything before the holder, and the fee it charges, is confirmed on this site.</li>
      <li>The rules are public, and every change to them is listed with its date.</li>
      <li>The builders will pick the winners, and every ballot will be published.</li>
      <li>Nobody will be disqualified without a written rule, a published reason and an appeal.</li>
      <li>When something here isn't true yet, the site says so.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
